                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "crossed-slit":</h3><p><a title="Neumann KM84 capsule backplate" rel="shadowbox" href="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/neumann/km84_capsule1.jpg"><img src="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/neumann/km84_capsule_100px.jpg" width="100" class="thumb shaft" /></a><b>Crossed-slit</b> is an informal description of a condenser microphone backplate manufacturing technique developed by Neumann in 1964. The term is translated from the German <em>Kreuzschlitz-Kapsel.</em></p><p><a title="Neumann KM84 capsule backplate" rel="shadowbox" href="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/neumann/km84_capsule2.jpg"><img src="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/neumann/km84_capsule2_100px.jpg" width="100" class="thumb shaft" /></a>Rather than rely on a precise arrangement of holes drilled into a brass plate to create the desired polar and frequency response, Neumann engineers discovered that by carving a series of intersecting slits or grooves, they could achieve even greater pattern control and more-linear frequency response.</p><p>The &#8220;crossed-slit&#8221; backplate construction technique was used on the Neumann KM84, KM83, and KM85, the current &#8220;Series 180&#8221; microphone capsules (KM 183, KM 184, KM 185), and the KM 100.</p><p><span style="font-family: Verdana,Sans-Serif; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">(Photo credit: Klaus Heyne / <a href="http://www.germanmasterworks.com/">GermanMasterworks.com</a>)</span></p>
